{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["And the reason for <b>and they shall fine him a hundred silver</b> [is] after the lashes required from <b>and they shall discipline him</b> (Ketubot 46), for the custom is to write for a virgin a Mohar of fifty silver, and behold this man brought an evil name upon her because he hated her and wanted to send her away with nothing, and therefore Scripture punishes him with a hundred silver, for the Torah punishes at double, as in the matter of 'he shall pay double' (Exodus 22:3). Even though the Ketubah is of rabbinic enactment for one who stipulates with her or marries her without specification, the custom was nevertheless to write a Mohar for virgins, as it is stated (Exodus 22:16) 'according to the Mohar of virgins,' and the majority would do so. And if there was no Ketubah for this woman, why would he fabricate charges against her because he had relations with her and hated her — let him write her a bill of divorce and be rid of her, as it is written (Malachi 2:16), 'for he hates, send away' — [n: Ramban reads the verse in Malachi 2:16 hyper-literally as a principle: since he hates her, let him send her away.]"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:53:48.494303456Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}